nyx-gestures 2.0.0

Touchpad gestures for GNOME, two interchangeable backends, one installer.
See README.md.

    daemon  libinput-gestures + a libei key injector. 2 processes, ~32 MB.
            Works on any GNOME version and any touchpad, including X11.
    ext     GNOME Shell extension. 0 extra processes, no key injection.
            Requires the compositor to expose get_gesture_motion_delta.

Neither backend patches a system binary. There are no apt hooks.

Platform selection
------------------
The installer probes the installed typelibs for get_gesture_motion_delta and
refuses the extension backend when it is absent, instead of shipping an
extension whose every swipe would silently fail. Measured:

    Ubuntu 26.04 / GNOME 50.1 / mutter 18   present  -> extension offered
    Ubuntu 22.04 / GNOME 42.9 / mutter 10   absent   -> extension refused

Note that the function is *declared* in mutter 10's C header but missing from
Clutter-10.typelib, so it is not reachable from GJS. The capability probe reads
the typelib, which is the same thing JS would see, rather than the C header.

Requirements
------------
daemon   python3, python-evdev, python-libei (installed into a private venv),
         xdotool as the X11 fallback. Root, for /usr/local.
ext      GNOME 45+. No root: the GSettings schema installs per-user.
both     a touchpad with at least 4 slots for the 4-finger gestures.

Logging
-------
Both halves write ~/.local/state/nyx-gestures/nyx.log at levels, rotated at
256 KiB. Change the level on a running session with:

    gsettings set org.gnome.shell.extensions.nyx-gestures level debug

Each gesture logs one line naming the action and whether it completed or was
declined, which distinguishes "the compositor never sent this gesture" from
"the action could not run".

